==Currency== <!--T:29-->

</translate><noinclude>{{CRT shortcut|CUR|Mexico}}
</noinclude><translate>
<!--T:30-->
{{NotMark}} Mexican currency is copyrighted and the reproduction of designs is only permitted by seeking authorisation from the Ministry of Finance (Secretaría de Hacienda y Crédito Público).</translate><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.banxico.org.mx/disposiciones/autorizaciones-consultas-y-sanciones/reproduccion-billetes-monedas.html |publisher=Banco de México |title=Reproduction of images of bills and coins|language=Spanish}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.diputados.gob.mx/LeyesBiblio/pdf/152.pdf |publisher=Cámara de Diputados |title=Ley Monetaria de Los Estados Unidos Mexicanos|language=Spanish}}</ref><translate>

<!--T:31-->
Coins and banknotes produced before 23 July 1928 entered the public domain before the law changed on 23 July 2003, and remain in the public domain. 

==Freedom of panorama== <!--T:32-->

</translate><noinclude>{{CRT shortcut|FOP|Mexico}}
</noinclude><translate>
<!--T:33-->
{{OkMark}} {{tlx|FoP-Mexico}} 

<!--T:34-->
Mexico's copyright law allows the reproduction of copyrighted works without compensation under certain circumstances, one of them being works that are visible from public places:

<!--T:48-->
:''Literary and artistic works that have already been disclosed may only be used in the following cases without the consent of the owner of the economic rights and without remuneration, provided that the normal exploitation of the work is not adversely affected thereby and provided also that the source is invariably mentioned and that no alteration is made to the work: [...]''
:*''Reproduction, communication and distribution by means of drawings, paintings, photographs and audiovisual processes of works that are visible from public places."''</translate>{{sref|1996-2018 Art.148(VII)}}<translate>

<!--T:35-->
The term ''public places'' has been interpreted to include both indoor and outdoor places that are open to the public. There is no legal secondary approved laws on the exact scope of the term.

<!--T:49-->
Independent of copyright law, the (government-owned) Mexico City Metro invokes its administrative authority to require permits for filming and photography inside its facilities.<tvar name="ref"><ref>https://www.metro.cdmx.gob.mx/tramites-y-servicios/toma-de-imagen-sin-fin-lucrativo</ref></tvar>

<!--T:50-->
The Mexican Federal Law on Telecommunications and Broadcasting also uses the term ''public places''. It defines public places as: "...those that are in the charge of dependencies of federal, state or municipal entities, or under public programs of any one of the three orders of government..." Under this law, public places include:

<!--T:36-->
* schools, universities, and every kind of building used for education;
* clinics, hospitals, and every kind of building used for health care;
* government offices of all types;
* community centers;
* places that are open to the public with free admission such as parks, green areas, and sports centers;
* places that collaborate in public federal programs.

<!--T:51-->
However, there is no source claiming that the term ''public places'' under Mexican copyright law can be interpreted in the same way.

